# CLI & TypeScript Conventions

## CLI design

- Commands should be composable and predictable (`init`, `scaffold`, `doctor`, `status`, etc.)
- Support both interactive and non-interactive / CI-friendly usage
- Prefer explicit flags over hidden behavior
- Include `--dry-run` for any command that mutates the workspace or overwrites generated files
- Help text should be concise and show the happy path first
- Prefer idempotent behavior: re-running scaffold should not thrash careful user edits without intent

## TypeScript

- Strict mode preferred
- Prefer interfaces / types that model the domain clearly (FactoryBlueprint, Stage, Recipe, SafetyRails, Profile, etc.)
- Avoid deep inheritance; favor composition
- Keep modules focused — one primary responsibility per file when practical
- Public CLI surface should be stable; internal modules can evolve faster

## Error handling

- Fail fast with clear messages
- Tell the user the next concrete action when possible
- Distinguish between user error (bad config, missing files) and system error

## Testing mindset

- Core schema validation, stage emission, and recipe loading should be unit-testable
- Prefer pure functions for blueprint → artifact transformation where possible
- CLI integration tests can come later; focus on the domain logic first
